course: dictionary
lesson: 8113
title: Reorganise: arranging things again
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To reorganise something means to arrange it again in a new way, usually a better way. The start re- means again, and organise means to put things in order. If your bookshelves are a mess, you reorganise them by putting the books in a new order that makes more sense.
explain: «We need to reorganise the shelves so the maths books are together.» The sentence tells you the shelves already have an order, and the plan is to change it. You can also reorganise your desk, your pencil case or your groups for a project.
explain: Groups and whole organisations can be reorganised too. A school or a club might change how it is run, for example who does which job. In maths, a messy list of numbers can be reorganised into a table or chart so it is easier to read.
fact: Reorganise means to arrange or plan something in a new and usually better way.
fact: «Reorganise the timetable» means change the order of lessons so the day makes more sense.
fact: Reorganise is a verb, so it changes: reorganises, reorganised, reorganising.
fact: In American English the spelling is reorganize, with a z.
fact: A company can reorganise too, and grown-ups sometimes call this restructuring.
exercise: «We will reorganise the shelves after lunch.» What does reorganise mean here? | Paint the shelves blue | *Arrange the shelves in a new, better way | Throw all the books away | Leave the shelves exactly as they are
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as reorganise? | Break | Forget | *Rearrange | Sleep
exercise: In maths, a messy list of numbers can be reorganised into a table. What happens to the list? | *It is arranged into a new, clearer form | It gets longer and messier | It becomes a song | It is thrown away
test: The class will reorganise into four new groups. What does reorganise mean here? | *Arrange the groups in a new way | Make everyone sit on the floor | Stop working together
test: Which spelling is correct in American English? | reorgainze | reorganyse | reorganise | *reorganize
exam: Dad says the company will reorganise its team, which means changing how it is run. Which meaning fits best? | Going on holiday | Making a new brand of cereal | *Changing how a group is arranged and run | Closing the company down for ever
